course: dictionary
lesson: 9221
title: One small word, many jobs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word some is small, but it can do several jobs. Most often it means a part of a group, not all of it. «Some birds can't fly» tells you that only certain birds cannot fly, not every bird.
explain: Look at the words after some to find its job. «Some water» or «some apples» means an amount you do not name exactly. When a number comes next, as in «some 20 children», some means about: roughly twenty children.
explain: Some can also mean a certain one you do not know or name, as in «Some boy left his coat.» In casual talk it can mean great or terrible, as in «That was some storm!» Read the whole sentence, then swap in a word like part of, about or great to check which job it does.
fact: Some means a part of a group, not all of it: «Some children are adopted» means only certain children.
fact: Some can mean an amount that is not said exactly, such as some water or some apples.
fact: Before a number, some means about: «some 20 people» means roughly twenty people.
fact: In casual talk, some can mean great or terrible: «That was some game!»
fact: Rude use: «Some idiot parked on the path» shows the speaker does not think much of that person.
exercise: «Some birds can't fly.» What does some mean here? | Exactly two birds | Very fast birds | All of the birds | *A part of the birds, not all of them
exercise: «We need some apples for the pie.» What does some mean here? | Exactly ten apples | *An amount that is not named exactly | Every apple in the shop | A certain boy called Apple
exercise: «Some 20 children joined the club.» What does some mean in this sentence? | Only twenty children, exactly | Some boy who joined late | *About twenty children | Not any children at all
test: «Some boy left his coat on the bus.» What does some mean here? | A great big boy | Every boy in the class | *A certain boy the speaker does not name | A part of the boys, not all of them
test: Jayden says, «That was some game!» What does some mean? | Finished early | *Great or very good | Terrible and boring | Not very much
exam: «Some hot springs smell of rotten eggs.» Which phrase best replaces some? | Each and every hot spring | A hot spring named Some | Exactly three hot springs | *A portion of the hot springs, not all of them
